iting `tests/test_budget.py` in this plan, so this belongs in a follow-up plan. Note that the 90 s and 240 s figures are unverified plan inputs.\n57\t\n58\t## Info\n59\t\n60\t### IN-01: Comment hardcodes unverified external figures\n61\t\n62\t**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-4`\n63\t**Issue:** The comment states the upstream failover is 90 s and the pager escalates at 240 s. These are copied from the plan, and the SUMMARY admits they were not independently checked. If either external value changes, the comment goes stale silently. \"Reports before the pager\" is also true only if the retry loop reports at the deadline, and `deadline_after` does not account for an in-flight call that outlasts the deadline.\n64\t**Fix:** Name the source of each figure in the comment (the upstream doc or pager policy). Alternatively, move both to named constants (see WR-02) so the relationship is checked.\n65\t\n66\t### IN-02: `deadline_after` does not state its clock domain or reject non-finite input\n67\t\n68\t**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n69\t**Issue:** The docstring does not say whether `start_secs` comes from `time.monotonic()` or `time.time()`. Mixing the two produces a deadline in the wrong time base. I ran it: `deadline_after(float('nan'))` returns `nan`, `deadline_after(float('inf'))` returns `inf`, and `deadline_after(True)` returns `121`. A NaN deadline makes any `now >= deadline` check always false, so a retry loop would never stop. No callers exist yet, so this is latent, not live.\n70\t**Fix:** Document the clock (prefer \"monotonic seconds\") in the docstring. Optionally guard with `math.isfinite(start_secs)` and raise `ValueError`.\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t_Reviewed: 2026-11-02_\n75\t_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_\n76\t_Depth: standard_\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"uuid-2","uuid":"uuid-168","timestamp":"2026-11-02T18:46:02.136Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"~/.cache/devflow-phase50.1/attempt-2/project/.worktrees/phas